#: processes/ui/staging-render-log.bpmn:67
#. Diagram element documentation
msgid "BEFORE the removal, not after: a job that dies mid-step must leave a record of the intent rather than an artefact that vanished with no entry. A reason is REQUIRED — an entry saying an artefact went and not why is the ambiguity this log exists to prevent."
msgstr ""

#: processes/ui/staging-render-log.bpmn:23
#. Diagram label
msgid "CI/CD Pipeline"
msgstr ""

#: processes/ui/staging-render-log.bpmn:85
#. Diagram label
msgid "deploy"
msgstr ""

#: processes/ui/staging-render-log.bpmn:81
#. Diagram label
msgid "Entry appended; history intact"
msgstr ""

#: processes/ui/staging-render-log.bpmn:24
#. Diagram element documentation
msgid "Every branch of GW_What — deploy, takedown, full-replace — is logged by this same lane regardless of which is firing, and the ORDER matters as much as the fact: A_LogRemoved happens before A_Remove specifically so a job that dies mid-step leaves a record of intent rather than an artefact that vanished with nothing said about it."
msgstr ""

#: processes/ui/staging-render-log.bpmn:16
#. Diagram element documentation
msgid "Every change to the publish branch appends an entry to `_render-log/<day>.jsonl`. A removal is a NEW entry, never the erasure of the `rendered` one before it — the pair is the history. The lane is the build pipeline because every writer is a workflow job; no agent decides what goes in this log. A system lane runs a fixed program and exercises no judgement, which is exactly what an append-only log wants. The question it exists to answer: what happened to branches/<slug>, and why. The publish branch has several publishers and one of them replaces the whole branch, which is why a removed preview needs a record of its own."
msgstr ""

